"""
grafana_metrics_util.py

Production-grade async utility to discover and fetch EVERY Prometheus metric
exposed via a Grafana-proxied (or direct) Prometheus HTTP API, scoped to a
given app_id / component / cluster / env, over an explicit epoch time range.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
ASSUMPTIONS (read before using -- these are the load-bearing design choices)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Label names: this util filters on Prometheus label keys `app_id`,
   `component`, `cluster`, `env`. If your metrics use different label keys
   (e.g. `application`, `svc`), change the LABEL_* constants below -- they
   are the single source of truth, nothing else needs to change.

2. grafana_url must be the BASE of a Prometheus-API-compatible endpoint,
   i.e. `/api/v1/...` can be appended directly. That means either:
     - a raw Prometheus URL, e.g. https://prometheus.internal:9090
     - a Grafana datasource proxy base, e.g.
       https://grafana.internal/api/datasources/proxy/uid/<datasource-uid>
   The Grafana *UI* URL (https://grafana.internal/d/...) will NOT work.

3. "All possible metrics" is implemented as: discover every metric name that
   has at least one series matching your label selector in the time window
   (via /api/v1/series), then fetch each one individually (via
   /api/v1/query_range). This is the only correct way to get this with the
   Prometheus HTTP API -- there is no single call that returns "all metrics
   + all data" for a label set.

4. cluster is restricted to {"man07", "gar07"} and env to {"nonprod"} per
   your environment. Both are simple allow-lists (ALLOWED_CLUSTERS /
   ALLOWED_ENVS) -- extend them in one place when you add clusters/envs.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
ROBUSTNESS CONTRACT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
- fetch_all_metrics() never raises for operational failures (auth, network,
  timeouts, bad PromQL, empty results, etc). It always returns a
  FetchAllMetricsResult with a `status` of "success" | "partial_success" |
  "error", plus structured error_code/message detail wherever something
  went wrong -- at the top level (validation_error / discovery_error) or
  per-metric (metrics[i].error).
- Only a genuine programming error (e.g. asyncio.CancelledError, a bug)
  will propagate as a Python exception.
- Transient failures (429, 5xx, timeouts, connection errors) are retried
  with jittered exponential backoff. Non-transient failures (401, 403, 404,
  malformed PromQL) are not retried.
"""

